The derivative of the linear function \(f(x) = -3x - 2\) is constant. Using the limit definition:

\[
f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{[-3(x+h) - 2] - (-3x - 2)}{h}
= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{-3x - 3h - 2 + 3x + 2}{h}
= \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{-3h}{h}
= \lim_{h \to 0} (-3) = -3.
\]

Alternatively, applying the power rule termwise:
\[
\frac{d}{dx}(-3x) = -3 \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{d}{dx}(-2) = 0,
\]
so the derivative is \(-3\).

\[
\boxed{-3}
\]
